    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I am looking into the thermal performance of our high-performance braking units. Specifically, I'm seeing some unexpected thermal instability during rapid deceleration cycles in our ceramic-matrix composite pads compared to the semi-metallic baseline. The heat dissipation seems inconsistent, and the braking feel becomes somewhat erratic once the rotors hit a certain temperature threshold. Can you explain the underlying thermodynamics of why ceramic pads might exhibit this kind of inconsistent thermal soak compared to semi-metallic options?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ceramic-matrix composites often have lower thermal conductivity compared to semi-metallic pads, which can restrict how quickly and uniformly heat is spread across the entire braking surface. This can lead to localized hotspots and uneven thermal soaking, contributing to inconsistent performance. Semi-metallic materials typically have better bulk thermal conductivity, allowing them to manage and dissipate peak heat loads more evenly during aggressive stops. The erratic feel you observe is likely linked to changes in the coefficient of friction as these localized temperature gradients destabilize the friction material's microstructure.",
